On 13 Apr 03, at 7:52, pa3gcu wrote:

> On Saturday 12 April 2003 08:08, David Jones wrote:
> > Current situation: Running three PCs. One 450MHz K- 6/2
> > with 384MB RAM and Linux. One 700MHz Duron with 512MB
> > RAM and OS/2. One 800MHz P3 with 512MB RAM and
> > Windows98SE.
> >
> > Proposed situation: One PC running Linux with
> > VirtualPC for Linux to run the other two OSes.
> >
> > Question: What processor speed and memory would be
> > needed to get equivalent performance out of OS/2 and W98
> > under VirtualPC on Linux? At the moment, I'm assuming
> > that maxing out the memory on the motherboard would be a
> > good place to start ...
> 
> No matter what speed the processor is you will never get a
> Virtual operating system to run at the same speed it would
> do when run on its own. However i use a Atlon 1800 with
> 512 MB and run win98 under VMware. I find win98's speed
> acceptable considering the limitations.

Sounds workable. I'm not looking to have Windows or 
OS/2 run at native speed for the faster new processor. 
Just as fast as what my current (signifcantly slower) 
hardware runs them. I'm not sure it would take much 
more processor speed to run an emulated OS faster 
than the K6-2 runs it native! ;-)

> > Related question: What Linux distro is considered the
> > fastest for desktop use, or are they all more-or-less
> > the same?
> 
> Its not a question of which linux-distro its a question of
> which window manager one uses. KDE and GNOME are well know
> as being memory hogs and basicly hogging system resources,
> they are of course the most widley used because they are
> very good and have a very nice and easy handeling. There
> are many lighter window managers around, icewm blackbox
> fvwm95 to name a few, but many are not as easy to use and
> configure as KDE and GNOME.

Yeah, I'd prefer to stick with KDE. IOW, pile on a lot of 
memory, eh?

